[TEXT] **Root cause found.** The `ip_addr_statistics` output delivers the smoking gun:

```
leaf_router_0_0:
  eth1: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>  state DOWN   172.16.0.9/31   ← NO UP/LOWER_UP flags

spine_router_0_1:
  eth1: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP>   172.16.0.8/31   ← fully UP
```

`leaf_router_0_0`'s **eth1 is administratively/operationally DOWN** while the spine side of the same link is healthy. This directly explains why the BGP session toward `spine_router_0_1` (which runs over that link) is stuck in **Active**: no TCP connection can be formed over a downed interface. `spine_router_0_1` still shows the session as Established because its BGP holddown timer (default 180 s) has not yet expired — only ~69 s have elapsed since the link dropped.

