=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The physical/virtual interface `eth1` on `leaf_router_0_0`, which is the point-to-point link connecting `leaf_router_0_0:eth1` (172.16.0.9/31) to `spine_router_0_1:eth1` (172.16.0.8/31).

**Mechanism:** `leaf_router_0_0:eth1` is administratively/operationally DOWN — the kernel reports state `DOWN` with no `UP` or `LOWER_UP` flags. Because the BGP session between `leaf_router_0_0` (AS 65200) and `spine_router_0_1` (AS 65101) runs directly over this /31 link, no TCP connection to 172.16.0.8:179 can be formed. FRR zebra detects the interface loss and immediately moves the BGP session to **Active** state (repeatedly trying to reconnect). On the `spine_router_0_1` side the BGP holddown timer (default 180 s) has not yet expired — only ~69 s elapsed — so `spine_router_0_1` still shows the session as **Established** with 172.16.0.9, a transient illusion that will self-correct once holddown fires. Meanwhile `spine_router_0_1`'s own `eth1` is fully `UP/LOWER_UP`, confirming the fault is one-sided.

**Localization:** `leaf_router_0_0`, interface `eth1` (172.16.0.9/31). All other interfaces on `leaf_router_0_0` (`eth0` toward `spine_router_0_0` and `eth2` toward `pc_0_0`) are UP and healthy, as are all interfaces on every other device.

**Root cause:** `leaf_router_0_0`'s `eth1` interface has been brought down (link_down fault), breaking the L2/L3 path to `spine_router_0_1` and cascading into the BGP session failure reported by monitoring.

**Fix:** On `leaf_router_0_0`, run `ip link set eth1 up` to restore the interface. Once the link is UP, FRR will re-establish the BGP session with `spine_router_0_1`, restore the alternate-path routes, and full ECMP across both spines will resume.
=== END ===